What are polyfills in javascript?

A polyfill is a piece of code that provides functionality on older browsers or environments that do not support the functionality natively. It "fills in" the missing features so that the code can run on older browsers or environments.

For example, if a browser does not support the fetch API, you can use a polyfill to provide the fetch functionality on that browser. The polyfill will detect that the browser does not support fetch, and then use a different method to make the request.

Polyfills are often used to provide support for new features in older browsers, or to provide support for features that are not yet widely supported. They can be useful for ensuring that your code works on a wider rangeeliof browsers and environments.

